  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Offering family planning services in factory settings in Egypt: Examining feasibility, acceptability, and cost</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Population Council/Egypt with support from the Netherlands (NL) Embassy) implemented a project to address family FP/RH needs of Egyptian factory workers through raising their awareness of the importance of FP and RH and providing them with on-site family planning services. The project objectives were to: (1) improve FP/RH-related knowledge, attitudes and behaviors of male and female factory workers in selected factories (2) assess the feasibility, acceptability, and effectiveness of offering on-site family planning information and services to factory workers, (3)measure the costs and potential return on investment of raising awareness of factory workers of importance of family planning within factory settings, and (4) document lessons learned from implementing this model of public &#x2013; private partnership, i.e., between Ministry of Health and Population (MOHP) and private factories.The intervention focused on male and female factory workers (both married and unmarried) and was implemented in 10 ready-made garment factories at the Free Zone of El Amereya, Alexandria governorate in partnership with the Egyptian Chamber of Apparel and Home Textile Industries (ECAHT) and in coordination with the Population and Family Development Administration (formerly Population and Family Planning sector) at the Ministry of Health and Population.</narrative>
  </description>
